WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.770
Tim Ferriss: Rich, it is a pleasure to have
you on the show.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.779
Thank you for making the time.

00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:19.360
Rich Paul: Thanks for having me, Tim.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.539
Tim Ferriss: And I thought we would begin
with something that came up in my research

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:26.949
and that is R&amp;J Confectionery.

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:32.140
Could you please explain for listeners what
R&amp;J Confectionery was?

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:38.500
Rich Paul: R&amp;J Confectionery was, to most
people, it was a store which my dad owned

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.500
and operated.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.230
It was a community store, sat on the corner
of 125th and Edmonton on the east side of

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.370
Cleveland in the Glenville area.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:53.110
For me, it was Harvard, Stanford, Penn.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.550
That's what it was for me.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.910
It was my curriculum, it was my education.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:06.369
It was where I was molded and built and taught
so many different things just through interaction

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:13.710
and also what I would say was just being able
to observe as well.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.150
Tim Ferriss: Now what types of things were
you observing?

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.970
What age were you when you first started spending
time there?

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.790
Could you just paint a picture for us?

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.909
Rich Paul: Yeah, I was very young.

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:27.900
I probably first started spending time in
the store when I was first able to walk, and

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.000
so a lot of the customers there watched me
grow up.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.870
And over time, the neighborhood obviously
changed, and so there were great days, fun

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.710
days, where you were a little kid running
around the store helping picking out candy,

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.509
eating candy, playing with your friends.

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:49.950
And then there were days in which you started
to see a difference.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:02:00.490
I started to see my friends' moms go from
this vibrant, beautiful person to strung out

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.230
on crack.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:12.190
I saw young men go from playing sports to
now interacting and engaging in things as

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.190
an adult.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:20.640
I saw a lot of violence, but at the same time,
it was where you got to observe and see how

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.000
people moved and people that you looked up
to or you thought were cool.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.930
And so it was just this combustion of activity
throughout the day.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:38.190
And my dad sat as the air traffic control
by him owning the store and things of that

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.190
nature.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:40.760
And so I learned a lot of what to do.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.100
I learned a lot of what not to do.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:51.400
I had several influences, both good and bad,
several instances in which I was able to probably

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:56.260
experience some things and here's some conversations
that at that age you probably shouldn't.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:03.050
But at the same time, when I look back on
it, I'm so glad I was able to because it really

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:06.540
put a lot of things in perspective, especially
for the seat I sit in now.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:14.549
Tim Ferriss: On the cover of your book, I
believe you are around 14, 13 or 14 years

00:03:14.549 --> 00:03:15.549
old?

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:16.549
Rich Paul: Yeah, that's correct.

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:22.140
Tim Ferriss: And I'm curious why you chose
that photo or that age for the cover?

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.209
Rich Paul: I think that's the age where in
life there's a fork in the road.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:34.549
And I think for me, and it's just in my experience,
that's the age in which a lot of my friends

00:03:34.549 --> 00:03:39.350
started off, started to go down a different
road.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:45.680
And that's the age for us growing up where
you actually feel like you are an adult, "I'm

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.800
going to make this decision," you're held
accountable for the Muny League football games

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.500
and the baseball games and things like that
for some are not as exciting.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.720
Now I want to make money, now I want to stay
out all night and just do certain things.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:08.049
And so that was the age when I had several
pictures to choose from, but I chose that

00:04:08.049 --> 00:04:13.569
picture because I think if you think about
even now in today's, especially in my community,

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:20.030
across America where I come from and all communities
like that, that age is just so important.

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:26.449
That's the age where you start to go down
one path or the other.

00:04:26.449 --> 00:04:29.010
And I started losing a lot of friends around
that age.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:34.449
Tim Ferriss: So let's hop to the present tense,
and we're going to go back to a lot of things

00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:38.970
that I want to ask you about, including James
Baldwin, who is endlessly interesting and

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.100
fascinating to me.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:45.900
But I would like to ask you about something
I know very little about, and that is the

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.919
free agency period.

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:57.440
So my understanding is that in the frantic
period that ensued this past July, you negotiated

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.030
something along the lines of close to $900
million worth of contracts.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:09.960
And I would love for you just to give us a
behind-the-scenes glimpse of how you prepare

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:17.540
for that and what that type of process looks
like once the clock strikes and the game is

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:18.540
on.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.440
What is that experience like for people who
don't have any familiarity?

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:28.220
Rich Paul: Well, for those who look at it
on ESPN or online, it's like, "Okay, it's

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.790
midnight July one, phone lines are open, everybody
can get to work."

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:41.070
For me and for us, my team, free agency doesn't
start July one in terms of your preparation.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:46.449
You start prepping beginning of the year probably
before the season, summer league.

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:49.390
There's a lot of things that you need to prep
for.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.600
There's a lot of posturing, there's a lot
of conversations, information, just watching

00:05:53.600 --> 00:06:01.250
what other teams do as well as development
and giving your guy some insights, as well,

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.650
so he can position himself better and understand
what the team is looking for or whatnot.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:10.940
And so by the time you get to July, you pretty
much have a decent board.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:15.419
There could be some times you don't know and
there's teams pop up out of nowhere.

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:20.830
But once that hits, now you're taking meetings
with different teams.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:26.420
And if you're lucky enough to have a guy that
has multiple teams, then he's trying to decide

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:32.340
what fits best for him and his family on one
end, and then you're trying to decide a number

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.949
on the other end with that team.

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:35.620
And so it's pretty hectic.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.900
It's a lot that comes with it.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.780
I know it looks easy, but it's not easy and
—

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:41.780
Tim Ferriss: It doesn't look easy.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:44.670
Rich Paul: And we were blessed to reach that
number.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:49.430
And I'm still in negotiations right now with
a couple of guys, and so it never ends.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:53.470
Tim Ferriss: So when you're doing the prep
and you say when you have a decent board,

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:59.129
and again, I apologized before we started
recording, but I'm coming in very uneducated

00:06:59.129 --> 00:07:00.639
with this type of thing.

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:02.080
I just don't have the familiarity.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.060
I followed a few sports, but mostly boxing
and fight sports.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:11.569
So with that in mind, what does it look like
to have a decent board in front of you in

00:07:11.569 --> 00:07:16.849
terms of what you've decided or strategy or
anything else?

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:17.849
What does that look like?

00:07:17.849 --> 00:07:22.020
Rich Paul: When you talk about a decent board,
I'm speaking about the options that you may

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.520
have based upon the players you represent.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.100
This year we had a number of players that
were sought after by a number of different

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.100
teams.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.300
And so when you have those options, that's
incredible.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.580
Any agent will tell you that's very hard to
do.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.360
And then it's just a matter of getting things
done in a timely manner.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.300
Teams, one thing about it, teams like to be
able to move.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:51.410
And so if you are moving your feet slow, they
have to jump to somebody else.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:56.180
Very few people, team's going to wait on,
very few people team's going to wait on.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:00.990
And so when you do have somebody that teams
are actually going to wait on, you also have

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:05.350
to balance that too because you want to be
respectful to them as well and their time.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.680
And so I try not to string people along.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.790
At the end of the day, you have to give yourself
time, but there's definitely a balance.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:21.610
Tim Ferriss: So I'd love to get into maybe
a story or some specifics of the negotiating

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:26.690
process because, by all accounts, you're considered
a master negotiator.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:31.659
And I'll give one example, maybe if you could
speak to this.

00:08:31.659 --> 00:08:37.630
How did information that you received negotiating
for Draymond Green's contract, which I think

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:42.399
was four years, 100 million, inform how you
then approached the market for Jerami Grant,

00:08:42.399 --> 00:08:45.150
which ended up being five years, 160 million?

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.930
I'm just wondering how things —
Rich Paul: Actually, Jerami's was done before

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:49.930
Draymond's.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:50.930
Tim Ferriss: Oh, okay, great.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.350
So let's —
Rich Paul: Jerami's was done before Draymond's.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:58.769
But to answer your question, Draymond was
in a much different situation than Jerami.

00:08:58.769 --> 00:09:05.060
Obviously he's older, he's been on the team,
one team for 11 years at the time.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:11.850
He's a guy that his stats may not necessarily
show his value, but when you plug him somewhere,

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.500
he brings a tremendous value.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:16.720
He brings a championship experience, a chip.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.899
He's one of the smartest guys on the court
every night.

00:09:19.899 --> 00:09:26.010
And so when you're negotiating for somebody
like Draymond, analytics and all those things

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.140
don't really matter.

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.490
In Jerami's case, we had already understood
what was happening in Portland and with their

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:40.030
star player at that time, but we also kept
the conversation, the communication, to understand

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:45.470
that they valued Jerami there as well, regardless
what was going on with their star player.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:46.630
So that was great to know.

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:50.630
And then there were some other teams around
the league that anybody can use a six nine

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:55.390
athletic wing like Jerami, versatile, the
whole nine.

00:09:55.390 --> 00:10:02.390
And it was a matter of if he wanted to stay
in Portland, despite what may have taken place.

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:05.500
And so, he did.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:09.340
And he had other teams with cap space that
would actually spend the money.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.300
Where Draymond was a little bit different.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.510
In order to get to his number, there was very
few teams with the cap space to do that.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:20.090
So now you've got to look at a sign and trade,
which we had a sign and trade lined up.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:24.149
But ultimately Draymond wanted to stay with
the Warriors.

00:10:24.149 --> 00:10:27.650
That's where he was drafted, that's where
he's had success.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:32.940
And so at that point, you just have to turn
and make the best deal with the team that

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:33.940
he was on.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:34.940
So that's what I was able to do.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:41.709
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to, and this may be
pushing for a tie-in, but I'm curious what

00:10:41.709 --> 00:10:46.130
type of gambling you were referring to when
you were younger.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:47.420
So when you were a kid?

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:49.500
Rich Paul: Yeah, we gambled on everything.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:52.040
If two people had a discrepancy, the next
word was bet.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.810
And so that was my mentality since I was seven,
eight years old.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:58.460
And so my dad taught me how to shoot dice
and play cards.

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:05.180
And then that led to horseshoes or betting
on any sports that you were capable of doing,

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:12.980
whether it was racing from end to end or whether
you decided to shoot jumpers at the park from

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:15.800
the top of the key like you would see on White
Men Can't Jump.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:21.320
We really did that at our park, and I practiced
it to the point to where I perfected it to

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.821
where I could shoot with one hand.

00:11:22.821 --> 00:11:27.899
And so allowing me to, if I'm betting a guy
and I'm going to shoot with one hand, then

00:11:27.899 --> 00:11:32.840
his bet has to be more than my bet because
I'm at a disadvantage, so to speak.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:38.290
So everything we did, whether it was ping
pong, it didn't matter what it was shooting

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.180
pool, I didn't want to play pool unless we
were betting.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.329
I didn't want to bowl unless we were betting.

00:11:42.329 --> 00:11:47.140
And so I just had that mentality growing up
that just everything was about a bet.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:48.450
So that's the type of gambling.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:53.380
That was street gambling prior to me playing
in the casino.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:56.380
But I don't really gamble as much at the casino
as I would in the streets.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:57.779
It's a totally different vibe for me.

00:11:57.779 --> 00:12:03.899
Tim Ferriss: So what are some of the things
that made you effective in street gambling?

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:08.640
And were there any particular games that were
your specialty or types of bets?

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:09.641
You mentioned the shooting.

00:12:09.641 --> 00:12:10.641
Rich Paul: Yeah, shooting dice.

00:12:10.641 --> 00:12:12.370
I was special.

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:16.329
Like Steph Curry has a great three-point shot,
I had a great shot.

00:12:16.329 --> 00:12:19.840
I was the Steph Curry of dice shooting, for
sure.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:21.510
But there's also a confidence.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:28.360
And there was also, for some weird reason,
I felt like I can actually talk to the dice,

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:29.649
and they could speak to me.

00:12:29.649 --> 00:12:33.030
It was my pet, everyone has a dog or a teddy
bear.

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:35.940
The dice was my pet.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.240
I would go around and just gamble.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.980
I would gamble against anybody.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.690
It didn't matter who it was.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:44.269
I would shoot dice against my grandmother
if she had some money and wanted to bet.

00:12:44.269 --> 00:12:46.670
And that was just my mentality.

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:47.670
So it was great.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:50.560
It was good though, but it was dangerous at
times.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:52.870
And I think about it all the time sometimes.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:57.170
Just being behind abandoned buildings or in
the basement of an abandoned house and you've

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:01.620
got this money and for things to go the way
they went for me, I'm extremely lucky.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:09.940
Tim Ferriss: And if we come back to the free
agency period, I'm curious if, for instance,

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:17.019
if you were for whatever reason sidelined
and you had to coach someone to step in your

00:13:17.019 --> 00:13:21.790
place to have a lot of the conversations,
to do a lot of the planning that you're talking

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:27.370
about, are there any key pieces of advice
that you might give them or warnings that

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:28.370
you might give them?

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:29.370
"Do not do this.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:30.370
I tried this once.

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:31.529
It's a bad idea."

00:13:31.529 --> 00:13:33.070
Any advice that you think would be key?

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:34.070
Rich Paul: There's several things.

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:36.690
You've got to really watch out for friendly
fire.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:40.170
There's a ton of friendly fire out there.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:44.990
Friendly fire in terms of giving too much
information to somebody, and then they share

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:47.449
that information, not knowing who they're
sharing it with.

00:13:47.449 --> 00:13:49.231
And it comes back to it backfires on you.

00:13:49.231 --> 00:13:50.231
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, bite you in the ass.

00:13:50.231 --> 00:13:51.910
Rich Paul: Yeah, that happens a lot.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:53.200
You've got to prepare.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:57.490
We've seen things happen this year with guys,
and it is not necessarily their fault.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:58.529
They wasn't prepared.

00:13:58.529 --> 00:14:02.930
You're going into a meeting with somebody,
you have to know pretty much what they're

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:08.720
looking for and being able to try to feel
the answer to the test already.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:17.029
Patience, can't panic, you can't take any
of it personal, it's not a personal thing.

00:14:17.029 --> 00:14:20.930
And I always say some people define the business
card and some people are defined by their

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:24.170
business card, and so I don't carry a business
card.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:27.320
So it puts things in perspective for you.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:31.110
But ultimately, every year I learn something
new.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.139
I'm a sponge man.

00:14:32.139 --> 00:14:34.040
I like to be educated.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:40.760
I have a different temperament than most would
probably have, and at the end of the day,

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.260
you're only going to be as strong as your
client.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:49.070
If you have a strong client, they trust you
and they are aligned.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:53.780
Then I've been in situations where a deal
got done in one day, and I've been in situations

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:58.329
where it took three and a half months and
I was fine with either one.

00:14:58.329 --> 00:15:01.630
Tim Ferriss: How did you first meet LeBron?

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:05.440
Rich Paul: We met in the airport, Akron County
Airport.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.720
Tim Ferriss: And what was the way in which
you guys met?

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:09.930
Did you go up to him?

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:11.079
Did he say something to you?

00:15:11.079 --> 00:15:12.079
Rich Paul: I had a jersey.

00:15:12.079 --> 00:15:15.790
I had a Warren Moon Oilers jersey on that
kind of caught his attention, and then he

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.810
asked me about the jersey and that's what
started the conversation.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:23.329
And then that led to us kind of bumping into
each other again at the baggage claim.

00:15:23.329 --> 00:15:27.970
And I gave him my business card because at
the time I did have a business card and I

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:31.971
was selling our jerseys to a store that he
can go to locally in Atlanta.

00:15:31.971 --> 00:15:37.230
And then when he got back to Ohio, they reached
out to me again and then that kind of started

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.230
the relationship.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:41.600
Tim Ferriss: How did the relationship evolve
from that point?

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:46.310
Rich Paul: I think just when I was young,
again, my life had really gave me so many

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:53.319
different tools and experiences and so when
you're a young kid like they were, I had all

00:15:53.319 --> 00:15:54.319
the things.

00:15:54.319 --> 00:15:59.230
I had the clothes, I had the jewelry, I had
the car, I had the know-how, I had the confidence,

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.370
I had the experience, and we would just talk.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:07.560
But I think the thing that drew us together
and closer was our moms.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:14.140
I was able to see him and observe things and
have conversations with him where he could

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:20.009
be himself and not feel the need to protect
himself because I was going through the same

00:16:20.009 --> 00:16:22.020
thing or I had gone through the same thing.

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.500
And so that's what really brought us closer
together.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:26.100
Tim Ferriss: Was the family similarity?

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:27.519
Rich Paul: Yeah, family similarity.

00:16:27.519 --> 00:16:28.519
Yeah.

00:16:28.519 --> 00:16:32.800
Tim Ferriss: I know I'm bouncing around a
lot, but that's sort of the nature of my mind.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:39.170
I'm trying to tease out some of the influences
or models that you've had.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:46.390
I know you have portraits of a number of different
people, and I understand one of those is James

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:47.529
Baldwin.

00:16:47.529 --> 00:16:55.060
And I was wondering if you could just explain
what the significance is of James for you

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:57.430
or why you're attracted to him.

00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:03.089
Rich Paul: Obviously, I didn't grow up with
him in that space, but just watching old videos

00:17:03.089 --> 00:17:08.910
and things like that, just what he stood for,
how articulate he was, how he saw things and

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:12.120
broke it down and the way he did it.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:17.309
I think it's important to really pay homage
to people like that.

00:17:17.309 --> 00:17:23.150
That room that picture's in, I have a number
of people from entertainers to activists to

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:31.809
actors from some of my favorite movies, both
mob movies and movies that was created by

00:17:31.809 --> 00:17:37.539
the John Singleton and Hughes brothers and
so on and so forth, as well as just different

00:17:37.539 --> 00:17:39.020
legends in music and sports.

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:41.710
That's a room of inspiration for me.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:50.660
But James Baldwin in particular, at a time
where it wasn't popular to be as blunt as

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:58.080
he was, it wasn't ideal for someone who looked
like him to be as smart and articulate as

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:02.059
he was and not just on the surface, but in
depth.

00:18:02.059 --> 00:18:09.880
That moved me and that was somebody that I
felt like I wanted to have on my wall.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:15.880
Tim Ferriss: I encourage everybody who's listening,
if you are not familiar with the name, look

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:16.880
up James Baldwin.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.300
I mean, so brilliant.

00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:28.590
So, as you mentioned, articulate and also
very good at presenting the messiness of life

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:33.880
in a way that wasn't over-indulgent, but very
truthful and bold, like you mentioned.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:38.179
And had a very complicated life himself, like
a lot of people.

00:18:38.179 --> 00:18:48.570
But I've collected quotes of his for the last
year or two, just an incredible depth of character.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:54.030
So I encourage people to check out James Baldwin,
if you haven't already.

00:18:54.030 --> 00:19:00.130
You have been incredibly successful in a world
that on some level, at least at points has

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:02.610
prioritized or highlighted formal degrees.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:05.520
But how how has that lens affected you?

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:11.390
Rich Paul: Well, I finished high school at
a very high level.

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:12.980
I just didn't need college.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:18.330
I actually made an attempt to go to college
and I actually enjoyed school.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:27.830
I really did, but my life was different and
for what I needed to do, I felt like I was

00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:28.830
prepared.

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:34.049
My dad did an unbelievable job in my environment,
did an unbelievable job of preparing me, and

00:19:34.049 --> 00:19:38.700
equipping me with the tools necessary for
me to persevere through life.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:45.950
I think society paints the picture that you
need a higher education for certain things,

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:51.490
and I think that when you go to get a job,
they go by what your education status is,

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:56.250
which again, for some jobs I understand it,
but most kids that graduate college, they're

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:59.429
not working in their field that they got a
degree in anyway.

00:19:59.429 --> 00:20:02.299
So I don't know how much of a — that actually
added —

00:20:02.299 --> 00:20:03.540
Tim Ferriss: Indicator, it is.

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:04.540
Yeah.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:05.540
Rich Paul: Yeah.

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:08.760
But I always felt prepared, especially for
what I do for sure.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:14.140
Tim Ferriss: So at some point, and again,
this is me just looking at some reading in

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:18.690
the process of preparing for this, but the
NCAA announced a rule at one point that agents

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:22.080
could not represent college athletes unless
they themselves had a college degree.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.360
Now at face value, that seems ridiculous.

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:32.030
And then LeBron dubbed on Twitter, this particular
regulation, the hashtag, the Rich Paul rule.

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:37.600
Why do you think they tried to put this into
action, and how did they justify it also?

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:39.140
Rich Paul: There was really no justification.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:46.250
I think they tried to put it into action to
prevent the next Rich Paul because it wasn't

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:53.059
about — as much as it was about me, it wasn't
nothing they could actually do to me, but

00:20:53.059 --> 00:20:55.260
they could discourage someone else.

00:20:55.260 --> 00:21:00.990
And it's no different than, again, you have
to have a college degree to become this person.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:08.780
Well, if you look at it and if you do the
data, that basically carves out a specific

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:15.380
group of people that are going to be given
opportunity based upon the completion of a

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.050
higher education.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:25.309
Because one thing about life, life don't always
afford you to pay attention to higher education,

00:21:25.309 --> 00:21:30.610
especially when you grew up the way I did
in terms of — and I know people had it worse

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:31.610
than I did.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:39.990
So when you talk about just trying to survive
and trying to survive the day, the week, the

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:47.380
month, younger siblings and lights are off,
gas is off, it is just a different dynamic,

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:54.930
and it's unfair in a lot of ways because you
basically feel trapped a lot of times.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:22:04.970
And so I feel like again, that's just a discouragement
and that's just an excuse to be able to say

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:09.820
why you didn't give somebody an opportunity
because they didn't have this.

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:15.730
But I think there's more people that can actually
learn on the job training, unless it's something

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:23.110
like you have to code at Microsoft or Apple
or you have to — and even that, there's

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:25.620
geniuses that learn this stuff at home.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:28.830
They're not learning it from a textbook.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:33.590
And so I just think it's all bullshit to be
honest with you.

00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:39.320
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I think it seems like people
came around on it to, whether they like to

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.320
or not, end up on the same page.

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:46.890
What would you say are any of the best investments
you've made?

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.400
And I'll explain what I mean.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:55.590
Because you have taken a very unorthodox path
to get to where you are, but you have studied

00:22:55.590 --> 00:23:01.850
and you've learned and observed like you mentioned,
from a very young age.

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.610
And so for instance, I'll just give a couple
examples.

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:08.110
Like Warren Buffett would say one of his best
investments, maybe his best investment, was

00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:12.830
taking a public speaking course, Dale Carnegie,
because it amplified everything he was able

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:13.830
to do.

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:17.961
A lot of people on the podcast have put time
into something or energy into something that

00:23:17.961 --> 00:23:21.090
paid dividends later.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:28.770
What have been some of the best investments
of time or energy or money that you've put

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:34.990
in in terms of something that laid the ground
for something that happened later, if that

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:35.990
makes any sense?

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:37.380
Rich Paul: For me, it was the dry runs.

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:45.370
Getting out on that road, driving to get in
front of a family and coming up short.

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:49.480
I think it really allows you to decide if
this is something you really want to do.

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:54.429
And if so, now I have to figure out ways to
get better because I didn't play the money

00:23:54.429 --> 00:23:55.429
game.

00:23:55.429 --> 00:23:58.620
When I got into this business, I didn't play
the money game.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:00.850
I didn't play the cut-your-fee game.

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:02.360
I didn't do any of that.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:05.010
All the stuff that's still going on now, I
didn't do that.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:11.429
And I was up against the bigger agencies that
had the perception, the cache, and they had

00:24:11.429 --> 00:24:19.240
the alliances within these universities and
things like that to help them, give them information,

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:24.010
give families the number or give the number
of families and then sit up a kid in a room

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:27.950
and tell them — don't tell them not to go
somewhere because they don't want that on

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:30.080
them, but they will tell them why they should
go here.

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:33.330
So now when you call the coach, they say,
"Well, I didn't tell them not to come with

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:34.330
you."

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:37.539
But you didn't tell them they should either,
but you told them they should for this company

00:24:37.539 --> 00:24:39.740
that you happen to be represented by and stuff
like that.

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:44.320
And so I had to deal with all those type of
things and navigate my way around that, which

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:45.320
I did.

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:47.590
But that was one of the best investments for
sure.

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:52.910
And then I think the other thing was just
kind of investing in self, knowing that and

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:57.480
believing that despite the shortcomings, just
stay at it.

00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:59.080
It'll turn.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:04.539
And again, in the dice game, we used to have
to be able to say, "Can you stand the rain?"

00:25:04.539 --> 00:25:09.520
A guy is throwing point after point after
point and you would have to stand in there

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:14.649
and stand the rain and eventually they turn
and things go the other way, so I was prepared.

00:25:14.649 --> 00:25:18.720
Tim Ferriss: So when you mentioned you weren't
playing the money game, do you mean that you

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:24.159
were not cutting your fees as a way to try
to make yourself more attractive?

00:25:24.159 --> 00:25:25.470
Could you say a bit more about that?

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:26.470
Rich Paul: Yeah.

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:30.289
Not cutting my fees is a way to make — because
people don't believe in your work practice

00:25:30.289 --> 00:25:31.289
and your expertise.

00:25:31.289 --> 00:25:35.399
And then the bigger companies, they're able
to do that because they're just rolling it

00:25:35.399 --> 00:25:38.690
all into their annual revenue, so if you've
got —

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:39.890
Tim Ferriss: Right, the larger machine.

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:41.470
Rich Paul: Yeah, 25 different verticals.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:49.780
Okay, so sports is one of them, and you roll
it into it and then they look at it as we'll

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:56.240
charge the higher less, we'll charge the middle
more and we'll charge the lower the max and

00:25:56.240 --> 00:26:01.340
they'll be appreciative to be here because
we probably shouldn't be representing them

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:02.340
anyway.

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:07.020
And then we'll roll it all up and at the end
of the day, it goes on our books and what's

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:08.760
dead is dead.

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:16.180
I have a clear understanding of the business,
but when you are on your own, you don't have

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:17.180
that.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:18.180
You can't do that.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:23.100
And so I was able to get players despite that,
and it actually drove them crazy and still

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:24.559
does to this day.

00:26:24.559 --> 00:26:27.309
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I bet it drove them crazy.

00:26:27.309 --> 00:26:32.340
So what were your selling points?

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:37.260
Because the big agencies have all of the stuff
that we could probably guess they would use

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:38.260
to sell.

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:42.080
We've got all this coverage, we've got all
these verticals, we can expand in these following

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:44.760
ways, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:50.530
What did you find over time as you're putting
in reps and learning, after coming up short

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:55.490
and then improving, what were your main selling
points that allowed you to get some of those

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:56.820
key early folks?

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:59.010
Rich Paul: I think there was a genuineness.

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:00.840
They can feel that in the room.

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:06.230
There was an actual real care for who they
were as individuals and as families.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:10.700
I just thought different — I led with education.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.870
I wasn't selling anything because I found
out very early, there's nothing to sell.

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:21.370
You can go in with your PowerPoint, back in
the day we put the CD in and show all these

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:25.190
other people that the company represented,
but the reality was those people were never

00:27:25.190 --> 00:27:27.910
going to touch those people.

00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:30.190
Those people don't actually care about the
people that's in this room.

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:36.190
So you're showing that as if it's all at one
family and it's not.

00:27:36.190 --> 00:27:45.429
And so I just really shined a light on that,
and the people that gave me an opportunity,

00:27:45.429 --> 00:27:50.059
I did right by them, which got me the next
opportunity.

00:27:50.059 --> 00:27:56.371
Tim Ferriss: You've said in a GQ interview,
which had a great title, "Rich Paul: Power

00:27:56.371 --> 00:28:02.510
Broker of the Year," that one of the biggest
obstacles for young players in learning how

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:05.970
to become a pro or be a pro is establishing
infrastructure.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:07.269
What do you mean by infrastructure?

00:28:07.269 --> 00:28:13.510
Rich Paul: Most guys, that's athletes especially,
they've come from an environment to where

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:16.620
everyone along the way has made it about them.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:22.779
And so if you have the mentality that it's
only about me, then you're not going to value

00:28:22.779 --> 00:28:27.000
other people and what their capabilities are,
what their expertise is.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:32.000
But if you are a walking corporation, we can't
name one corporation that does not have infrastructure.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:33.000
Organization.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:39.159
They have an organizational chart, they have
people that do certain things and there's

00:28:39.159 --> 00:28:43.310
clarity within that infrastructure, but it
comes with the costs.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.399
And so most athletes don't want to do what?

00:28:45.399 --> 00:28:51.580
They don't want to pay anybody to do anything
because along the way, everyone has did things

00:28:51.580 --> 00:28:55.890
for them for free, but it wasn't really for
free.

00:28:55.890 --> 00:29:01.990
It was to be able to be standing there next
to them at a time where there was actually

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:03.669
something to gain.

00:29:03.669 --> 00:29:08.740
So I'll sacrifice this now when you're an
amateur to be able to stand next to you to

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:13.059
get some crumbs or whatever the case when
you become a pro.

00:29:13.059 --> 00:29:19.690
And whatever their aspirations were, if it
was to hang out, the girls, to travel, whatever

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:24.880
it is, the money, whatever it was, but you
see what they did.

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:32.280
Basically, you created something that ultimately
became your own demise because up front you

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:33.309
taught them wrong.

00:29:33.309 --> 00:29:35.760
You taught them, "I'm doing this out of love.

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:38.190
I'm going to pick you up, I'm going to do
this, I'm going to do that."

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:41.529
All to turn it around on them when they start
making money and say, "Remember when I did

00:29:41.529 --> 00:29:43.190
this or I did that?"

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:47.900
Well, that's not fair to the player, and it's
also not fair to yourself.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:54.149
What the conversation should be like is, "Look,
I understand you don't have the ability to

00:29:54.149 --> 00:29:56.580
pay for anything, and if you're a parent,
I'm doing it out of love.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.580
I love you.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:29:58.880
You're my kid."

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:01.570
But if you're somebody else, just be honest
with it.

00:30:01.570 --> 00:30:06.650
"Hey, look, I'm going to do all I can to help
you get to where you need to go.

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:12.460
Once you get there, if there's an opportunity
for me, then I'm going to do all I can to

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:18.299
position myself to where if you give me that
opportunity, I can then be of value to you."

00:30:18.299 --> 00:30:19.480
Very simple.

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:24.980
Tim Ferriss: When free isn't free, right,
it's an unclear prepayment for things to come

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:28.610
later, that's when it seems like things can
get extremely messy.

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:35.190
How do you help them when they, say, sign
with you, or even just start to develop a

00:30:35.190 --> 00:30:40.019
relationship with you, to take the first steps
for building out infrastructure?

00:30:40.019 --> 00:30:43.350
What are some of the first steps that you
might recommend?

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:48.400
Rich Paul: I would say checks and balances.

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:55.140
Really getting the proper financial team in
place, and that's everybody from a CPA to

00:30:55.140 --> 00:31:01.600
the guy who manages your investment portfolio
or the company that manages your investment

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:02.600
portfolio.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:09.190
Really having somebody monitor that because
that's a very fragile thing there.

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:12.750
But at the same time, deciding what it is
that you want.

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:19.320
I like to live a certain way now that I understand
better on how to live.

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:27.320
And so if you are a single man with no family,
do you really need a 20,000 square foot home?

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:28.320
Probably not.

00:31:28.320 --> 00:31:31.029
You're not going to be home half the time.

00:31:31.029 --> 00:31:32.640
Do you really need to fly private?

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:35.279
Probably not.

00:31:35.279 --> 00:31:39.200
So there's different ways to go about it,
but what's most important?

00:31:39.200 --> 00:31:40.380
Should you hire a chef?

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:42.120
Yes, you should.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:43.529
Should you take a shortcut on that?

00:31:43.529 --> 00:31:44.950
No, you shouldn't.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:47.389
Do you know the difference between a chef
and a cook?

00:31:47.389 --> 00:31:50.171
You should find that out because this is very
important.

00:31:50.171 --> 00:31:54.040
Your chef should then go and sit with your
nutritionist on your team and figure out.

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:58.669
You should do all the testing, everything
you need, because your body ultimately is

00:31:58.669 --> 00:32:00.929
the engine that keeps you running.

00:32:00.929 --> 00:32:02.480
That's how you make your money.

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:12.360
Tim Ferriss: What are some of the key ingredients
for emotional support and stability?

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:18.179
I'm just imagining young athletes who are
suddenly in the limelight, they have all the

00:32:18.179 --> 00:32:21.320
temptations, they have a lot of pressure.

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:26.480
There must be, and I know a few professional
athletes who've gone through these periods,

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:30.720
periods that are very challenging, and certainly
after retirement or the end of their playing

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:33.610
years, some very challenging times.

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:38.260
What have you seen in terms of the best ways
to support that?

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:43.809
Could be from your side, it could be other
ingredients, player, health on that side of

00:32:43.809 --> 00:32:44.809
the ledger.

00:32:44.809 --> 00:32:45.809
Rich Paul: Yeah.

00:32:45.809 --> 00:32:48.240
I think nowadays, I think there needs to be
someone that players need to talk to and be

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:51.980
open-minded to having a therapist to get stuff
off their chest because they deal with a lot.

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:53.990
They deal with a lot.

00:32:53.990 --> 00:32:58.230
I think it's also important for players to
not feel entitled to do things.

00:32:58.230 --> 00:33:04.120
Oftentimes, they feel like they have to take
on all of this onus to do something for other

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:05.679
people, and that's not real.

00:33:05.679 --> 00:33:10.870
Because when it comes down to it, if you ask
probably 80 percent of the players that aren't

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:15.389
in the position they used to be in and ask
them who can do for them, there's not going

00:33:15.389 --> 00:33:18.929
to be many, if any for that matter, and so
—

00:33:18.929 --> 00:33:20.200
Tim Ferriss: Meaning people who —
Rich Paul: People, yeah —

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:21.200
Tim Ferriss: — are willing to help them?

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:22.200
Rich Paul: Yeah, of course.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:23.200
Yeah.

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:24.200
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:27.330
Rich Paul: Then how are they positioning themselves
to where when the ball stops, it's not about

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:31.279
just having a ton of money like, "How can
I do something else?

00:33:31.279 --> 00:33:36.870
How can I be perceived, while playing, as
someone who can transition and do something

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:37.870
else?"

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:41.510
There's this idea that, "Oh, I made a lot
of money, I don't have to do nothing else."

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:46.750
Well, that's not true because you made a lot
of money, but you didn't diversify your portfolio.

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:51.710
Unless your money is making money for you
and you live a lifestyle in which your eight

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:57.779
percent or your 10 percent or whatever it
is return each year is able to pay your bills

00:33:57.779 --> 00:34:04.720
and your taxes on your home and stuff like
that, then you've got to go to work.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:07.539
You have to go to work.

00:34:07.539 --> 00:34:12.980
It's a big difference when you've made $50
million this year and then next year you made

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:13.980
nothing.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:14.980
It's just tough.

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:15.980
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:21.179
What do you say to the players when they start
to take on that onus you were mentioning,

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:22.179
right?

00:34:22.179 --> 00:34:26.620
So people come to them and ask them for things,
I'm sure it happens all the time from many

00:34:26.620 --> 00:34:31.750
different directions, from people they've
met along the way, from new people.

00:34:31.750 --> 00:34:38.220
When you see someone who's about to take on
too much or accept that, what does that conversation

00:34:38.220 --> 00:34:39.220
look like?

00:34:39.220 --> 00:34:43.090
Rich Paul: If they come to me with the conversation,
it's very real.

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:45.940
I don't really have a lot of gray in me.

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:47.109
It's pretty black and white.

00:34:47.109 --> 00:34:51.690
Even though I have a gray Klutch Athletics
hoodie, it's pretty black and white for me

00:34:51.690 --> 00:34:53.230
because that's how life is.

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:56.260
Life is, there's no gray in it, man.

00:34:56.260 --> 00:35:03.000
Outside of the days, 300 of the days in Cleveland,
Ohio, that's great, but there's no real gray

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:04.110
in it, man.

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:11.630
I just want them to understand how important
it is to get their ducks in a row at their

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:15.380
youngest age, because that's when they make
their money at their youngest age.

00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:19.010
The average person makes their money between
the age of 45 and 65.

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:24.090
The average athlete makes their money between
the age of 19 and 25, maybe 30 if they get

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:25.329
that far.

00:35:25.329 --> 00:35:27.540
It's so hard.

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:32.079
Then learning when to jump off that dream
train, right?

00:35:32.079 --> 00:35:35.080
Yes, I know you thought you was going to be
a Hall of Famer.

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:36.820
You thought you was going to be an all-NBA
guy.

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:43.020
You thought you was going to play 15 years
in the league, but if you play two or three

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:46.010
or five, save your money.

00:35:46.010 --> 00:35:51.770
Do the things while playing to learn how this
business actually operates and start to position

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:56.890
yourself to where I can still be around the
game in a different form.

00:35:56.890 --> 00:36:04.160
When you look at guys, and there's a reason
why top coaches aren't really ex-players or

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:10.050
ex-star players, let me say that, ex-star
players because they wasn't allowed to have

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:14.980
the ego that most star players have, and so
they actually become better coaches.

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:19.190
When you look at a Ty Lue, when you look at
a guy like Adrian Griffin, who's getting the

00:36:19.190 --> 00:36:23.680
opportunity now in Milwaukee, well deserved,
overly deserved.

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:33.380
When you look at guys like Quin Snyder, who—
Quin played ball, he was a good player.

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:35.310
He wasn't John Stockton, right?

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:36.310
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:41.990
Rich Paul: But he's a hell of a coach, and
he's going to make more money as a coach than

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:43.670
he would as a player.

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:45.670
Ty Lue has already done it.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:47.430
Darvin Ham's going to do it.

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:52.990
When you really think about this, because
I studied this stuff, it's a guy like Jamahl

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:53.990
Mosley.

00:36:53.990 --> 00:36:57.480
He tried to play ball and played ball a little
bit, but whatever.

00:36:57.480 --> 00:37:03.630
But as a coach, he's going to be — but he
paid his dues, went from development to this

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:06.800
to that, and just have the time.

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:11.810
Give yourself time and be patient, and do
the little things necessary to continue to

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:15.360
reposition yourself and it's going to happen
for you.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:21.730
That can be any part of the ecosystem of sport,
in any sport for that matter.

00:37:21.730 --> 00:37:29.810
Tim Ferriss: So you mentioned ego, and I'd
love to focus on this for a second because

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:36.609
it seems like LeBron has never been known
as someone who's ego-driven.

00:37:36.609 --> 00:37:43.180
I'd love to hear your thoughts on this, of
course, but as someone that talented and that

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:51.960
driven, obviously putting in intense amounts
of work and dedication, one could imagine

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:56.390
a world in which a person like that would
be very ego-driven, and certainly there are

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:59.180
counter examples.

00:37:59.180 --> 00:38:04.510
What do you think it is that has led him to
be that way, or is he just that way right

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:05.510
out of the box?

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:07.320
Rich Paul: I think he's that way.

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:10.180
But also, LeBron's from Akron, Ohio.

00:38:10.180 --> 00:38:15.920
If you've ever been to Akron, there's not
much there.

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:25.390
So there wasn't these things that allowed
him to develop this ego, per se.

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:30.540
When you think about his friends, same guys
he went to high school with, same guys he

00:38:30.540 --> 00:38:32.520
go back with now.

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:37.220
Yeah, he's LeBron, but they don't give a — they
still talk crazy to him, and they talk about

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:43.000
things and they laugh and joke or whatever
and fall out and get back together.

00:38:43.000 --> 00:38:44.010
That's just life.

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:49.369
Then when you think about his business infrastructure,
myself, Maverick, Randy, et cetera, we don't

00:38:49.369 --> 00:38:53.250
really care that he's LeBron, per se.

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:56.380
He values our opinion, we value him.

00:38:56.380 --> 00:39:02.319
If we have a discrepancy or disagreement or
something, we're not going to hold our tongue

00:39:02.319 --> 00:39:07.660
because he's LeBron, and that's one aspect
of it, but that's not even what's the most

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:08.810
important.

00:39:08.810 --> 00:39:16.869
The most important thing in all of that, though,
is he's not going to look at it as if he can

00:39:16.869 --> 00:39:22.819
overpower or just do something for the sake
of doing it because he is LeBron.

00:39:22.819 --> 00:39:28.349
See, it takes two to tango, and that has helped
him along the way.

00:39:28.349 --> 00:39:34.990
We all have disagreements in meetings and
so on and so forth, but one thing about him,

00:39:34.990 --> 00:39:43.160
he's egoless enough to know when he should
listen.

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:48.579
That's just showing people respect and believing
in them, and so I think that's helped him

00:39:48.579 --> 00:39:49.579
tremendously.

00:39:49.579 --> 00:39:50.579
Now, we all have ego.

00:39:50.579 --> 00:39:55.160
Don't get me wrong, you have to have an ego
to have confidence, but you're not invested

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:56.160
in your ego.

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:58.599
You're not leading with the ego.

00:39:58.599 --> 00:40:01.700
You know when the ego is supposed to come
out the room and when it's not.

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:06.160
When he's at that—I still call it the Staples
Center—he has an ego.

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:07.230
He's LeBron James.

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:08.300
That's his show.

00:40:08.300 --> 00:40:09.300
That's his stage.

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:14.020
He's there to perform and put on a great show,
sometimes too great of a show if you ask me.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:19.589
I would like him to be a little bit more precise
with things, and we debate about that as well.

00:40:19.589 --> 00:40:24.180
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to talk about how you
think about luck.

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:26.680
So the name of your memoir is Lucky Me.

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:27.680
Rich Paul: Mm-hmm.

00:40:27.680 --> 00:40:28.849
Tim Ferriss: Why Lucky Me?

00:40:28.849 --> 00:40:30.280
Rich Paul: Why Lucky Me?

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:34.369
Look, when you think about it, I grew up in
my dad's store.

00:40:34.369 --> 00:40:40.040
I learned math through playing the numbers
and selling beer, wine, cigarettes, et cetera,

00:40:40.040 --> 00:40:41.040
candy.

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:42.390
I was extremely lucky to have that environment.

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:49.970
I was extremely lucky to have a dad who believed
in me enough to stress education and to teach

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:55.420
me things in which that he knew I was going
to need to be successful in life; not successful

00:40:55.420 --> 00:41:00.520
for the moment, but successful for the duration,
and success not necessarily meaning from a

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:05.010
finance perspective, but just from a humanity
perspective, extremely lucky.

00:41:05.010 --> 00:41:10.130
I was extremely lucky to go through some of
the things that I went through to help shape

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:16.470
me and mold me to understand life's challenges
and to be able to survive those thunderstorms

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:20.160
emotionally and things like that, to get out
on the other side.

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:27.550
So in addition to Jay-Z's Lucky Me being my
favorite record, there's a number of things

00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:28.800
that I was extremely lucky for.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:31.690
I was able to make it out of an environment
to where many didn't.

00:41:31.690 --> 00:41:36.680
When I say they didn't, not because they're
dead or in jail, but because mentally, they

00:41:36.680 --> 00:41:40.490
just can't see past what's in front of them.

00:41:40.490 --> 00:41:45.160
I'm extremely blessed and lucky to be able
to do so to the point to where it's gotten

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:46.390
me this far.

00:41:46.390 --> 00:41:51.569
At the same time, it's a little sarcasm to
it because look, I didn't have my mom.

00:41:51.569 --> 00:41:53.810
I didn't have many options.

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:59.920
My entrepreneurial spirit led me down the
road of what some would say was detriment

00:41:59.920 --> 00:42:00.920
and darkness.

00:42:00.920 --> 00:42:03.609
At the same time, I was able to persevere
through it.

00:42:03.609 --> 00:42:10.470
So there's a number of meanings in between
it or amongst it, but ultimately, I felt that

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:11.470
was the right title.

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:12.550
Tim Ferriss: A great title.

00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:18.859
Could you say a bit more about the entrepreneurial
impulse leading you into darkness, if I heard

00:42:18.859 --> 00:42:19.859
you correctly?

00:42:19.859 --> 00:42:21.190
Can you say a bit more about that chapter?

00:42:21.190 --> 00:42:23.960
Rich Paul: Growing up, we had very little
options.

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:30.720
If you had an entrepreneurial spirit, you
only could be that entrepreneur through hustles.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:38.120
That hustle could have been everything from
selling jerseys to selling drugs, selling

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:44.980
candy to selling inner tubes from a bike or
everything was a hustle.

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:46.600
There was no real jobs.

00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:51.090
There was no real understanding of corporate
America.

00:42:51.090 --> 00:42:56.400
There was no opportunities and there was no
examples, more importantly, to see someone

00:42:56.400 --> 00:43:03.369
go to work every day, get a raise, get promoted,
and then go from being promoted, get promoted

00:43:03.369 --> 00:43:05.869
again, and then become partner.

00:43:05.869 --> 00:43:08.240
We didn't have these examples that I have
today.

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:13.430
Tim Ferriss: We flash back because it seems
like, at least based on some of the notes

00:43:13.430 --> 00:43:18.510
I have in front of me, one of the through
lines for you, one of the themes is, possibly,

00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:21.970
"How you do one thing is how you do everything."

00:43:21.970 --> 00:43:30.230
I wanted to ask about creasing and ironing
your jeans as a kid.

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:31.420
Is that something that you did?

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:33.380
If so, why did you do that?

00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:36.901
Rich Paul: My sister taught me how to iron
my jeans first and foremost.

00:43:36.901 --> 00:43:41.119
Then once she taught me, it made me feel like
I was an adult at a very young age.

00:43:41.119 --> 00:43:47.890
Then I started to decide that there's the
detail in it and ironing my jeans the way

00:43:47.890 --> 00:43:51.860
I did and having the creases line up, and
then I perfected that.

00:43:51.860 --> 00:43:56.319
Then from there, I realized that, "Oh, I can
iron really good."

00:43:56.319 --> 00:44:00.350
So I started to advertise my ability to iron.

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:03.510
So when I would go to my uncle's house or
somebody's house and they're getting dressed

00:44:03.510 --> 00:44:07.500
to get ready to go to the bar or the club,
and they can't do two things at once.

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:11.390
So I say, "Hey, I'll iron your clothes for
you, and there's going to be a charge for

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:12.390
that."

00:44:12.390 --> 00:44:15.010
I made money ironing clothes with no problem.

00:44:15.010 --> 00:44:22.190
I think that the detail of it and my preciseness
of it allowed them to trust me more.

00:44:22.190 --> 00:44:24.760
The more they trusted me, the more I can charge.

00:44:24.760 --> 00:44:30.609
So now ironing jeans is one thing, but if
I'm ironing silk pants or a silk shirt and

00:44:30.609 --> 00:44:35.490
I knew how to adjust the iron to wool and
linen and so on and so forth, and I built

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:39.400
a good business, and I made money every which
way like that.

00:44:39.400 --> 00:44:45.990
But then me understanding that and willing
and wanting to align those creases properly

00:44:45.990 --> 00:44:53.339
and take my time to do it and prepare as such
with the iron and the water and light starch

00:44:53.339 --> 00:44:58.200
and things like that, that quote that you
made, "How you do one thing is how you do

00:44:58.200 --> 00:45:03.290
everything," that's at its foundation and
your approach to it.

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:10.740
Because whatever arena you're in, just your
approach to that, that detail, staying that

00:45:10.740 --> 00:45:14.990
detail-oriented will allow you to progress.

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:16.520
So that's what that meant.

00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:20.730
Tim Ferriss: Usually, I wouldn't ask about
something in the background, but I have to

00:45:20.730 --> 00:45:24.990
ask, what is the polar bear statue in the
background behind you?

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:25.990
Looks like a polar bear.

00:45:25.990 --> 00:45:28.470
Rich Paul: Oh, oh, oh, oh, yeah, that's just
interior design.

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:30.550
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:45:30.550 --> 00:45:32.540
I have a thing for polar bears, that's why
I was asking.

00:45:32.540 --> 00:45:35.050
Rich Paul: Yeah, but that's an Ernie Barnes
behind me, that picture.

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:36.050
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:37.050
Rich Paul: Yeah.

00:45:37.050 --> 00:45:39.430
Tim Ferriss: So can you tell me more about
the Ernie Barnes piece?

00:45:39.430 --> 00:45:41.700
You seem like somebody who chooses things.

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:44.040
I imagine these things aren't just thrown
in your house.

00:45:44.040 --> 00:45:45.140
Rich Paul: Oh, no.

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:47.589
No, they're placed properly.

00:45:47.589 --> 00:45:54.540
Ernie Barnes is a well-known Black artist.

00:45:54.540 --> 00:45:58.200
He's most known for the cover of Marvin Gaye's
album and —

00:45:58.200 --> 00:45:59.520
Tim Ferriss: I recognize the style.

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:01.490
I've never seen that piece, but I recognize
the style.

00:46:01.490 --> 00:46:04.220
Rich Paul: The picture at the end of Good
Times, that painting is called "The Sugar

00:46:04.220 --> 00:46:05.220
Shack."

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:11.490
Eddie Murphy actually owns the original one
from my understanding, and I was fortunate

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:15.200
enough to be able to purchase this piece.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:17.560
This is called "The Runway."

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:26.220
So growing up, I watched Good Times a lot,
didn't know anything about art, saw that picture

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:27.220
the whole time.

00:46:27.220 --> 00:46:29.839
Then as I got more into art, I started to
learn different artists, and Ernie Barnes

00:46:29.839 --> 00:46:33.020
was somebody I wanted to make sure I had in
my collection.

00:46:33.020 --> 00:46:35.130
Tim Ferriss: What is player empowerment?

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:36.490
What does that mean?

00:46:36.490 --> 00:46:41.300
Rich Paul: Player empowerment, I know that
people use this a lot, but it's really player

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:43.050
choice.

00:46:43.050 --> 00:46:48.300
I think that oftentimes people say as player
empowerment, you get confused with like, "Oh,

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:49.839
a player can just do whatever they want."

00:46:49.839 --> 00:46:51.809
No, that's not the case.

00:46:51.809 --> 00:46:58.240
I think player empowerment comes through the
lens of education, understanding that you

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:08.829
do have a choice, flexibility, and just having
a mindset that "I don't necessarily have to

00:47:08.829 --> 00:47:16.410
play my career or put my career on the same
track as somebody else's because that's what

00:47:16.410 --> 00:47:22.579
the media or anybody around my game would
expect me to do."

00:47:22.579 --> 00:47:26.050
And the example of that is Reggie Miller was
a great player.

00:47:26.050 --> 00:47:29.319
He spent his whole time with the Indiana Pacers.

00:47:29.319 --> 00:47:31.100
Dirk Nowitzki was a great player.

00:47:31.100 --> 00:47:32.500
He spent his whole time with the Mavericks.

00:47:32.500 --> 00:47:35.480
Kobe was a great player, he spent his whole
time with the Lakers.

00:47:35.480 --> 00:47:38.790
But LeBron and KD are great players.

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:40.280
They've been on several teams.

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:47.570
That doesn't make them any lesser than a legend
or icon as those other guys that just named.

00:47:47.570 --> 00:47:56.559
But because these people get on these media
platforms and try to create a narrative of

00:47:56.559 --> 00:48:00.820
what's right and what's wrong and how something
should go versus how it shouldn't.

00:48:00.820 --> 00:48:03.740
No, everybody's career and their journey is
their journey.

00:48:03.740 --> 00:48:08.819
But I think it's a misconception with player
empowerment because it's not like they can

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:11.510
write their own checks or their own teams.

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:15.820
So it's only so much power within that empowerment.

00:48:15.820 --> 00:48:23.200
But I think the empowerment is for players
to give, to empower other players to do as

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:33.010
you feel necessary within the lines of your
professional positioning and obviously your

00:48:33.010 --> 00:48:39.890
contract, but don't feel the need to have
your journey look like someone else's.

00:48:39.890 --> 00:48:48.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, self-authoring with choice
and educated choice, like you mentioned.

00:48:48.280 --> 00:48:53.570
Who were the most important influences in
developing your confidence?

00:48:53.570 --> 00:48:57.630
Rich Paul: Again, going back to my dad, first
and foremost.

00:48:57.630 --> 00:49:02.859
My brother, who even to this day, when I called
my brother about something, he's like — and

00:49:02.859 --> 00:49:07.799
I'll be talking to him like, "Bro, I'm thinking
about doing this and what do you think?

00:49:07.799 --> 00:49:09.829
I think it may work."

00:49:09.829 --> 00:49:12.170
He's like, "Bro, it's going to work.

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:14.770
I've been telling you this since you was a
kid.

00:49:14.770 --> 00:49:15.890
It's going to work.

00:49:15.890 --> 00:49:16.890
Don't even worry about it."

00:49:16.890 --> 00:49:19.100
This is his exact conversation.

00:49:19.100 --> 00:49:22.420
But outside of family, I've had a lot of people
that instilled confidence in me.

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:28.540
My Uncle Warren when I played mini football,
they would put the confidence in you.

00:49:28.540 --> 00:49:33.720
You may not like the words, but you know that
they instill the confidence in you.

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:35.599
Tim Ferriss: What kind of things might he
say?

00:49:35.599 --> 00:49:36.670
How did he do that?

00:49:36.670 --> 00:49:37.670
Rich Paul: Who?

00:49:37.670 --> 00:49:38.670
My brother and my Uncle Warren?

00:49:38.670 --> 00:49:39.670
Tim Ferriss: Your uncle.

00:49:39.670 --> 00:49:40.670
Yeah.

00:49:40.670 --> 00:49:44.440
Rich Paul: I mean, I played quarterback when
I was a Muny League football player, or if

00:49:44.440 --> 00:49:46.859
I switched to receiver or safety.

00:49:46.859 --> 00:49:50.310
He would just, "You know what you're supposed
to do, you know how to do it.

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:51.310
We worked on it.

00:49:51.310 --> 00:49:53.089
You know what we practice on, you can do it."

00:49:53.089 --> 00:49:55.040
And that was the mentality.

00:49:55.040 --> 00:50:02.200
We had the expectation of when Belichick was
leading the Patriots, when the Patriots were,

00:50:02.200 --> 00:50:05.100
if they didn't want to go to the Super Bowl,
it was a failed season.

00:50:05.100 --> 00:50:06.290
That was our mini league team.

00:50:06.290 --> 00:50:09.910
If we did not go to the city championship,
something was wrong.

00:50:09.910 --> 00:50:16.580
And so you go into practice with that understanding
and that mentality and that expectation on

00:50:16.580 --> 00:50:20.270
you, that's what it was.

00:50:20.270 --> 00:50:25.609
And even when I was young, gambling, shooting
dice, my dad would instill that confidence

00:50:25.609 --> 00:50:26.980
in me.

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:30.210
I've had some unbelievable comeback.

00:50:30.210 --> 00:50:36.920
You think the Cavs came back from 3-1, you
don't know what it's like to have a hundred

00:50:36.920 --> 00:50:45.369
dollars and you down to your last dollar and
you're 11 years old and you've got $1 left,

00:50:45.369 --> 00:50:50.839
and you turn that dollar into 200, you roar
all the way back.

00:50:50.839 --> 00:50:52.119
It's the —
Tim Ferriss: Redemption trail.

00:50:52.119 --> 00:50:53.400
Rich Paul: For sure.

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:56.560
It's an unbelievable feeling to do that.

00:50:56.560 --> 00:51:02.420
And I had plenty of nights like that, whether
it was on the basketball court or whether

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:06.950
it was at the dice game, and I think about
those moments all the time.

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:08.440
Those were some fun times, man.

00:51:08.440 --> 00:51:09.440
It really was.

00:51:09.440 --> 00:51:10.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:11.440
Yeah.

00:51:11.440 --> 00:51:12.440
Formative times.

00:51:12.440 --> 00:51:13.440
Yeah.

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:14.440
Why do a book?

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:15.440
There's so much involved.

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:20.650
You have to prioritize it, you have to promote
it.

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:21.890
Why do a book?

00:51:21.890 --> 00:51:26.800
What does it mean to you and what do you hope
it will do in terms of impact for people who

00:51:26.800 --> 00:51:27.800
read it?

00:51:27.800 --> 00:51:32.460
Rich Paul: I think a book brings a seriousness
to it.

00:51:32.460 --> 00:51:34.510
Instead of just doing a visual.

00:51:34.510 --> 00:51:39.550
I think when people really sit down and read
a book, you can really dive into its chapters

00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:42.420
and read it again and go back.

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:45.549
And so the book form was important.

00:51:45.549 --> 00:51:48.280
The timing of it was important.

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:50.110
You look at the state of the world.

00:51:50.110 --> 00:52:00.310
How our youth sees the world today, the different
perceptions that people may feel like they

00:52:00.310 --> 00:52:05.360
have without being able to have someone hear
them.

00:52:05.360 --> 00:52:11.550
I wanted to write a book to where I shared
my experiences, I shared my journey, but also

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:18.160
wanting to let people know that you're being
heard, and not only are you being heard, you

00:52:18.160 --> 00:52:21.660
can be in a different place as long as you
stay in the moment.

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:29.609
I know it may seem dark and gray and bleak
and challenging, but you tend to think that

00:52:29.609 --> 00:52:33.349
everyone's paying attention to you and what
you've got going on.

00:52:33.349 --> 00:52:37.910
And so you get discouraged, but in reality,
they're not.

00:52:37.910 --> 00:52:40.069
Everyone has their own problems.

00:52:40.069 --> 00:52:41.069
Just get through yours.

00:52:41.069 --> 00:52:42.069
Tim Ferriss: Totally.

00:52:42.069 --> 00:52:45.620
Rich Paul: Get through them, continue to push,
continue to persevere, and you can end up

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:46.620
in a different spot.

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:51.230
I wanted to give kids that example and just
people in general.

00:52:51.230 --> 00:52:55.339
Regardless of race, regardless of gender,
I just wanted to give you that example.

00:52:55.339 --> 00:53:00.910
And it was the right time for me because I'm
at a place to where I'm honestly comfortable

00:53:00.910 --> 00:53:07.930
enough, and I feel like I've accomplished
enough to allow people in because today people

00:53:07.930 --> 00:53:09.790
want to be Rich Paul.

00:53:09.790 --> 00:53:12.820
They want to be a better version, and they
want to do the things that I do.

00:53:12.820 --> 00:53:14.880
They want to be an agent because of me.

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:23.609
Once I got to that point, I wanted to help
them understand who I really am and why I

00:53:23.609 --> 00:53:24.940
do the things I do.

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:29.109
Because it's very misleading to say, "Oh,
this guy became a top agent.

00:53:29.109 --> 00:53:32.180
He'd done $4 billion in contracts and he didn't
go to college."

00:53:32.180 --> 00:53:35.799
Well, that could —
Tim Ferriss: There's more to it.

00:53:35.799 --> 00:53:40.700
Rich Paul: Yeah, that could leave a kid like
he playing Mortal Kombat, it's like just sitting

00:53:40.700 --> 00:53:43.119
there like dizzy.

00:53:43.119 --> 00:53:52.840
I wanted to give them an example and an understanding
and paint a picture through storytelling that

00:53:52.840 --> 00:53:59.101
they see themselves in that same mirror and
like, "Damn, I don't have to go out here and

00:53:59.101 --> 00:54:00.101
rob somebody.

00:54:00.101 --> 00:54:01.660
I don't have to go out here and kill somebody.

00:54:01.660 --> 00:54:04.890
I don't have to go out here and feel like
I'm not being heard.

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:06.450
He had the same problems I did.

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:07.450
Wow.

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:08.490
I see myself in that."

00:54:08.490 --> 00:54:14.099
And here's an example that I see every day.

00:54:14.099 --> 00:54:16.140
I can see him, I can touch them, whatever.

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:17.250
I'm accessible to people.

00:54:17.250 --> 00:54:19.900
I like to talk to people when they see me
out in the street and it's like, wow.

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:21.359
So that was encouraging for me.

00:54:21.359 --> 00:54:23.329
What I didn't want to do is write a puff piece.

00:54:23.329 --> 00:54:24.650
And that's what I said to my team.

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:27.809
I said, "If I do a book, it will not be a
puff piece.

00:54:27.809 --> 00:54:30.510
I don't want to write a book on, 'Look what
I did.

00:54:30.510 --> 00:54:32.170
Look how much money I made.'

00:54:32.170 --> 00:54:33.829
I don't need to do that."

00:54:33.829 --> 00:54:35.599
That could be the second book.

00:54:35.599 --> 00:54:36.609
That can't be the first book.

00:54:36.609 --> 00:54:41.450
The first book, people need to know who I
am at my core, what I've been through.

00:54:41.450 --> 00:54:44.079
And it was therapeutic for me, and I'm glad
I did it.

00:54:44.079 --> 00:54:46.250
It was something that I'm proud of.

00:54:46.250 --> 00:54:50.270
And hopefully when people read the book, they
feel inspired by it.

00:54:50.270 --> 00:54:52.280
Tim Ferriss: And the subtitle is great also.

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:53.960
I mean, I love everything about it.

00:54:53.960 --> 00:55:00.730
I mean, Lucky Me: A Memoir of Changing the
Odds, and certainly you've demonstrated that.

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:03.599
And just a few closing questions for you.

00:55:03.599 --> 00:55:09.620
The first is, if you could put anything on
a billboard, this is metaphorically speaking,

00:55:09.620 --> 00:55:13.780
just to get a message out to billions of people.

00:55:13.780 --> 00:55:18.589
Could be an image, could be a quote, could
be a word, could be something that's inspired

00:55:18.589 --> 00:55:20.549
you, anything at all.

00:55:20.549 --> 00:55:22.240
What might you put on that billboard?

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:23.559
Do you have any ideas?

00:55:23.559 --> 00:55:27.720
Rich Paul: I would put, "Kindness never hurts.

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:28.960
Love is never wasted.

00:55:28.960 --> 00:55:31.680
Unity starts with you.

00:55:31.680 --> 00:55:33.329
Trust makes it possible.

00:55:33.329 --> 00:55:37.590
Community is a mentality, and honesty keeps
it real."

00:55:37.590 --> 00:55:49.720
And I think you need all those things to change
the way people are living today for the better.

00:55:49.720 --> 00:55:54.480
You need all those things to help uplift somebody.

00:55:54.480 --> 00:56:02.260
You need all those things to unite and elaborate,
and you need all those things to be Klutch.

00:56:02.260 --> 00:56:05.549
Tim Ferriss: Rich, I've really enjoyed spending
time with you.

00:56:05.549 --> 00:56:11.589
People can find you on Instagram @RichPaul,
Twitter @RichPaul4, the number four, and certainly

00:56:11.589 --> 00:56:15.730
they can find the new book Lucky Me: A Memoir
of Changing the Odds, everywhere fine books

00:56:15.730 --> 00:56:16.730
are sold.

00:56:16.730 --> 00:56:22.080
Is there anything else you'd like to say or
anything you'd like to ask of my audience?

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:24.780
Anything at all you'd like to add before we
wind up?

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:27.710
Rich Paul: I want to thank you for having
me on.

00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:33.220
I know you're not into sports like that, but
obviously I'm doing something right to get

00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:35.319
your attention, so I'm happy for that.

00:56:35.319 --> 00:56:39.190
And to the audience, you don't have to be
in sports, don't feel like I have to be in

00:56:39.190 --> 00:56:40.410
sports to read this book.

00:56:40.410 --> 00:56:44.400
You don't even have to know how to spell sports
per se.

00:56:44.400 --> 00:56:51.230
And I think that it's important to really
care about what other people are going through

00:56:51.230 --> 00:56:53.650
despite what society teaches us.

00:56:53.650 --> 00:57:02.200
I think it's important to really dive into
other people's experiences because it allows

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:09.230
you to try to have a better perspective and
a better understanding of people.

00:57:09.230 --> 00:57:16.040
I'm glad to have matured to the point to where
I can honestly say that, because I grew up

00:57:16.040 --> 00:57:18.990
in an environment where we didn't care about
nothing at all.

00:57:18.990 --> 00:57:28.829
Tim Ferriss: Well, Rich, I can barely spell
sports, but I can spell human, and your story

00:57:28.829 --> 00:57:31.180
is intensely human.

00:57:31.180 --> 00:57:39.040
I think it will resonate with a lot of readers
and it's a hero's journey of changing the

00:57:39.040 --> 00:57:40.040
odds.

00:57:40.040 --> 00:57:43.200
I'm very excited to see what it'll do in the
world.

00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:46.150
Lucky Me: A Memoir of Changing the Odds is
the book, everyone.

00:57:46.150 --> 00:57:47.680
Please check it out.

00:57:47.680 --> 00:57:49.710
And thank you so much for taking the time,
Rich.

00:57:49.710 --> 00:57:51.170
Rich Paul: Thanks, Tim, I appreciate it.

00:57:51.170 --> 00:57:54.859
Tim Ferriss: This has been a lot of fun.

00:57:54.859 --> 00:57:58.250
And to everybody listening, we'll include
links in the show notes as usual to everything

00:57:58.250 --> 00:57:59.250
at tim.blog/podcast.

00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:04.930
Until next time, be just a bit kinder than
is necessary to others and to yourself, and

00:58:04.930 --> 00:58:05.910
thanks for tuning in.

